## Structural Patterns

### Adapter

**Intent:** Make incompatible interfaces work together through a translation layer.

**Modern form:** Wrapper function, extension method, protocol conformance shim.

**Use when:**
- Integrating with a third-party API you cannot change
- Replacing a dependency while keeping the client unchanged
- Translating between internal and external data shapes

**Do not use when:**
- You can change one of the interfaces directly
- The adaptation logic belongs in the source or target
- You are adapting your own new code (redesign instead)

### Bridge

**Intent:** Decouple an abstraction from its implementation so both vary independently.

**Modern form:** Dependency injection with interfaces, trait objects, generics with bounds.

**Use when:**
- Both the abstraction and implementation have meaningful variation axes
- You need to swap implementations at runtime without touching the abstraction
- Platform-specific or provider-specific logic must be isolated

**Do not use when:**
- Only one axis varies (use a simple interface)
- The abstraction and implementation are tightly coupled by design
- You are anticipating future variation that has not appeared

### Composite

**Intent:** Compose objects into tree structures so individual and composite objects are used uniformly.

**Modern form:** Recursive data types, algebraic data types with sum types.

**Use when:**
- The domain naturally has part-whole hierarchies (UI trees, file systems, ASTs)
- Operations apply uniformly to leaves and branches
- Clients should not distinguish between individual and composite

**Do not use when:**
- The hierarchy is artificial (forced to make things uniform)
- Leaves and composites have fundamentally different semantics
- A flat collection with grouping logic is simpler

### Decorator

**Intent:** Attach additional responsibilities to an object dynamically, without modifying its class.

**Modern form:** Middleware, higher-order functions, extension methods, AOP decorators.

**Use when:**
- Cross-cutting behavior (logging, caching, retry, auth) wraps core behavior
- Combinations of enhancements must be configurable at runtime
- Inheritance would create combinatorial class explosion

**Do not use when:**
- The decoration is always applied (just build it in)
- A single concern can be handled by a simple wrapper
- The language supports aspects or interceptors natively

### Facade

**Intent:** Provide a simplified interface to a complex subsystem.

**Modern form:** Module-level convenience functions, simplified API exports.

**Use when:**
- A subsystem has a steep onboarding cost for common use cases
- Multiple clients need the same sequence of subsystem calls
- You want to isolate clients from subsystem internals

**Do not use when:**
- The subsystem is already simple
- The facade duplicates the subsystem API one-to-one
- You are hiding complexity that callers should understand

### Flyweight

**Intent:** Share fine-grained objects to support large numbers efficiently.

**Modern form:** Interning, string interning, object pools, value type caching.

**Use when:**
- Memory or allocation cost is measured and significant
- Many objects share identical intrinsic state
- The language does not already intern or cache the type

**Do not use when:**
- The objects carry significant extrinsic state
- Modern allocators handle the volume efficiently
- A simple map or cache would suffice

### Proxy

**Intent:** Control access to an object through a surrogate.

**Modern form:** Lazy evaluation, memoization, virtual proxies via language features.

**Use when:**
- Object creation is expensive and may not be needed (virtual proxy)
- Access must be controlled (protection proxy)
- Remote access needs local representation (remote proxy)
- Caching results avoids repeated expensive computation (cache proxy)

**Do not use when:**
- The language supports lazy evaluation natively
- Access control can be handled by visibility modifiers
- The proxy adds indirection without measurable benefit

